WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.212
The English Premier
League is widely regarded

00:00:11.212 --> 00:00:14.802
as being home to the very
best soccer in the world.

00:00:14.802 --> 00:00:15.643
It's a goal!

00:00:15.643 --> 00:00:18.418
Stanislas makes it three-three!

00:00:19.492 --> 00:00:22.159
It's watched by billions across the globe,

00:00:22.159 --> 00:00:24.800
making it the richest too.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.217
And while soccer,

00:00:27.261 --> 00:00:29.519
as we Americans call it

00:00:29.519 --> 00:00:32.210
isn't one of the top
sports here in the U.S.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:36.777
it's never seen so many
American investors in the U.K..

00:00:36.777 --> 00:00:38.270
Wait!

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.354
Even American superheroes are getting

00:00:40.354 --> 00:00:41.412
in on the action.

00:00:41.412 --> 00:00:42.505
Cue the music.

00:00:43.595 --> 00:00:45.145
Could Deadpool and Mac

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:48.718
from 'It's Always Sunny'
really save the day?

00:00:48.718 --> 00:00:51.527
US sports teams bring in
a huge amount of money

00:00:51.527 --> 00:00:52.650
for their owners.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.380
But if you wanna buy into the NFL

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.600
from the Dallas Cowboys to
the lowly Cleveland Browns,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:58.740
sorry Cleveland,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:02.283
you probably need to be a
billionaire several times over.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.750
But, for those who love what the rest

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.340
of the world calls football,

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.821
buying into the English
Premier League is way cheaper

00:01:10.821 --> 00:01:13.696
and could give you a much better return.

00:01:13.696 --> 00:01:16.129
Aguero!

00:01:16.129 --> 00:01:19.330
A billion plus people
interact with the EPL.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:20.921
When you look at any American sport

00:01:20.921 --> 00:01:22.171
there's nothing anywhere close to it.

00:01:22.171 --> 00:01:24.375
It just smelled of opportunity to me.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.710
While the huge investment

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:29.920
in the EPL is intrinsically linked

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.460
to the high quality of the competition

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.891
and its star names, big money talks.

00:01:35.891 --> 00:01:38.950
And it doesn't care much
for history or tradition.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:42.240
As radical plans to transform
the game have been revealed

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.740
U.S. investors are increasingly
viewed with suspicion.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.273
But will these changes be
for the good of the game,

00:01:48.273 --> 00:01:49.964
or just the wealthy owners?

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:04.289
Takes the snap,

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:05.122
drops back,

00:02:05.122 --> 00:02:06.131
pumps once, looks right

00:02:06.131 --> 00:02:06.964
throws, endzone!

00:02:06.964 --> 00:02:08.077
Jets have the lead,

00:02:08.077 --> 00:02:10.019
it's 54, 35.

00:02:12.453 --> 00:02:14.170
Dover Athletic nil,

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:15.812
Northwich Victoria nil.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.040
While our sporting sensibilities

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.440
might be drastically different,

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.560
when it comes to sheer
commercial enterprise,

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.250
the English Premier
League is right up there.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.500
The Premier League's
broadcast revenue is more

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.320
than four billion dollars a season.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.270
It's way richer than the
other European leagues,

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:38.170
even the ones with global
superstars, like Messi,

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:40.369
Ronaldo and Neymar.

00:02:40.369 --> 00:02:42.694
And more of us in the
States are tuning in too,

00:02:42.694 --> 00:02:45.490
at the expense of our
own pro soccer league.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.170
More than twice as many
people watch EPL games

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:49.750
than the MLS.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.470
So how did English football
become such a hot deal

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:54.410
for American investors?

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.830
I need an Englishman to
help me figure this out.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:00.380
All right, so Alex, educate me here.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.180
As an American I'm intrigued,

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.320
but also a little bit confused, candidly,

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.540
by English football.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.770
So, what are the main differences,

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.350
especially from the business perspective?

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:15.070
Fundamentally, in the U.S. you
have a league system whereby

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.800
there's a defined number
of teams in the league.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.240
Revenues tend to be doled
out a lot more evenly

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:21.080
between those teams.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.050
Obviously if you finish a
little bit higher at the table,

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:24.010
you get a little bit more.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:27.740
In Europe, particularly in the
U.K. you have a league system

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.390
of four professional divisions

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.340
and than a number more amateur divisions.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:33.690
And theoretically, if you or I set up

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.670
an amateur team tomorrow, we could,

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:38.337
inside a decade, be playing
in the Premier League.

00:03:38.337 --> 00:03:40.860
Obviously in practice
it's a lot more difficult

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.080
but by being promoted up the tiers,

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.513
we could be a Premier League team.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:49.110
At 11:30 precisely the game gets underway

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:50.700
with Chelsea attacking.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.020
It's left winger Tommy Knox
working the ball along,

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.390
across to Harris, then
to right winger Murray.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:56.770
Straight into the safe hands of England

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:59.390
and Blackpool goalkeeper, Tony Waiters.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:00.430
A lot of the history

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.000
of these teams is so baked into

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.160
the community, maybe
in a way that it isn't

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:07.460
here in the United States.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.610
Yeah, when you look at the
names of teams, actually,

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:10.860
you could see some of that.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:12.560
Think about a team like Arsenal.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.130
It was actually the team associated

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.300
with the Woolwich
Arsenal, a military base,

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:19.890
south of the river in London.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.840
With Aston Villa, that's my team,

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.727
it was a cricket team
that met up and said,

00:04:24.727 --> 00:04:26.570
We want to stay fit during the winter.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:29.210
They met outside Aston Villa,

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:32.020
a villa in the Birmingham suburb of Aston,

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.300
named their team after it.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.680
This is a massive business.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:37.960
I mean the reason that this is attracting

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.660
so much investment ultimately is

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.370
because these team values
have just skyrocketed,

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:46.140
certainly, in the EPL.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:49.548
Yes and a lot of that has
to do with the success

00:04:49.548 --> 00:04:51.540
of cultivating an international audience.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.680
The way that the Premier
League in particular

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:55.310
has really forged a lead

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:58.408
was in a sense by following
what happened in the U.S..

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:06.390
In the early '90s they started
doing collective bargaining

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:07.650
for their broadcasting rights.

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:09.620
It used to be the case that
if you're Manchester United

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:11.530
or Arsenal, or whichever club it might be,

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:14.933
you would individually
negotiate with the broadcaster.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.617
They said, actually, we're
better off as a unit,

00:05:18.617 --> 00:05:20.190
if we all negotiate together.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:22.402
And that's when the
Premier League was formed.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.960
You tie that up with the
arrival of Rupert Murdoch,

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:30.603
trying to bring a satellite
TV station to the U.K..

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:33.890
He recognized there was
a huge amount of passion

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:34.940
for football.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:36.780
If he could lock up the rights for that,

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:40.189
he would secure an audience
for his pay TV station.

00:05:40.189 --> 00:05:41.902
The F.A. Premier League,

00:05:41.902 --> 00:05:44.930
live, only on Sky.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:46.960
Now since the Premier League was formed,

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:48.980
we've seen a huge ballooning

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:51.050
in the value of broadcast rights.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:54.500
It's jumped 60-fold since 1992.

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:56.580
So previously the value of the rights was

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:58.920
about £600,000 per game.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:02.570
In the most recent round it
was about 9 million per game.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:04.680
That is astonishing.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:06.860
So, with those sorts of figures,

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:10.630
it stands to reason that big
investors in the United States

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:12.990
and elsewhere would come calling

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.860
and see a lot of opportunity.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:18.543
They've seen opportunities for a bargain.

00:06:20.178 --> 00:06:22.240
You can buy a middling football club

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.240
for several hundred million.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.610
You can buy a football
club in the second tier,

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:27.890
for considerably less than that.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:30.444
You compare that to the
NFL, where, you know,

00:06:30.444 --> 00:06:33.900
franchises change hands for
perhaps two billion dollars.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:35.560
Huge difference there.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:37.662
We're not for fucking sale!

00:06:37.662 --> 00:06:39.360
We're not for fucking sale!

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.560
The most famous U.S.
owners were the Glazers

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:43.280
who owned the Tampa Bay Bucs.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:44.940
They came and bought Manchester United.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:47.520
And they approached it
with the kind of attitude

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:48.770
of a private equity company.

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:51.610
You can pile a huge
amount of debt onto it,

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:54.400
extract some of that as cash

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.320
to line your own pockets

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.970
and because they are
fundamentally media assets,

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:00.920
it's quite resilient.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.110
As long as they invest
enough in the playing squad,

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:04.500
keep them in the Champions League,

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:06.270
you're going to be able to generate enough

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:09.212
free cashflow to service
your debt obligations.

00:07:10.961 --> 00:07:12.819
This is a couple of
thousand quid's worth of

00:07:12.819 --> 00:07:15.335
season tickets going up in smoke

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.470
The Glazers aren't exactly
loved in Manchester

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:24.628
and to many British fans,
they reflect the confusing

00:07:24.628 --> 00:07:27.019
emotions inspired by U.S. ownership.

00:07:27.019 --> 00:07:30.380
Incredible financial
growth might also coincide

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:32.523
with declining sporting performance.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:37.920
If you're looking for a model

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:39.723
of American ownership to follow,

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:45.763
it might just be Man United's
biggest rivals, Liverpool.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:49.940
The current champions
have enjoyed a resurgence

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:50.840
in recent years.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:52.860
And that's happened since
they were taken over

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:54.410
by Fenway Sports Group.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:56.803
Those are the guys behind
the Boston Red Sox.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:01.870
An ownership group that
knew what success was about,

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:05.661
that knew how to build patiently,

00:08:05.661 --> 00:08:07.686
everything that a football club is about.

00:08:07.686 --> 00:08:08.620
This is Peter Moore,

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:10.300
he was the CEO of Liverpool

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:12.775
when they won the Champions League in 2019

00:08:12.775 --> 00:08:15.841
and then the Premier League in 2020.

00:08:15.841 --> 00:08:18.650
So Peter, what did Fenway
bring to Liverpool?

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:20.300
Maybe something specific that they brought

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.170
from running a very successful

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.630
and very fabled American baseball team.

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:26.300
First thing that struck me when I spoke

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:28.247
with Mike Gordon is, he said,

00:08:28.247 --> 00:08:30.160
"We're not the owners
of this football club."

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:33.158
He said, "We have the
economic rights to do business

00:08:33.158 --> 00:08:35.777
on behalf of this football club.

00:08:35.777 --> 00:08:37.287
The owners are the fans."

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:42.130
For me certainly there
was no true interference

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:44.220
in how we managed the football club.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:46.180
We improved the structure
of the football club,

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:48.000
they applied science and data.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.280
All of the things that they'd learned

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:53.350
in the days of Theo Epstein
and young general managers.

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:56.780
And brought in actual
scientists and data analysts.

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:59.670
So the thousands of players were analyzed

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:01.380
that were in the marketplace.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:03.120
And our own players were analyzed

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:04.898
to get the best out of them.

00:09:05.765 --> 00:09:07.601
Quick snap, protected in the pocket

00:09:09.001 --> 00:09:10.021
the ball comes out!

00:09:10.021 --> 00:09:12.357
And the 49ers are on it!

00:09:12.357 --> 00:09:15.100
One of the EPL's newest
American execs comes

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.610
with a deep knowledge
of American football.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.750
And a business acumen shaped by decades

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:21.820
in and around Silicon Valley.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:24.510
But he's the first to
admit he's not a soccer fan

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:25.393
from the cradle.

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:31.070
I started looking into
soccer clubs, football clubs

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.400
in Europe, gosh, maybe 10 years ago.

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:35.440
At that time, you know,
with all due respect,

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:38.910
I still didn't know if a soccer
ball was puffed or stuffed.

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:40.620
Paraag Marathe is the president

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:42.500
of 49ers Enterprises,

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:45.640
which bought a stake in
Leeds United in 2018.

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:47.180
Part of an ambitious effort by one

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:51.170
of America's story franchises
to expand its portfolio.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:54.530
At the time Leeds was
itself a fabled franchise.

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:56.880
But one that had fallen on hard times.

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:58.860
Leeds were playing in the second division

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:00.460
of English football.

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:03.310
Last season they won
promotion to the top division,

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:04.143
the EPL.

00:10:10.126 --> 00:10:11.965
Premier League here we come!

00:10:11.965 --> 00:10:13.180
Come on!

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:14.440
So tell me about promotion

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:15.770
from a business perspective

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:18.210
because you're doing a calculation here

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:20.380
about a huge potential upside.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:24.500
You're talking about a 20X increase

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:26.720
in per club media rights

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:28.634
from going from the Championship
to the Premier League.

00:10:28.634 --> 00:10:31.690
You still play in the same
stadium with the same size.

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:32.523
And you have the same number of fans.

00:10:32.523 --> 00:10:34.970
I can count the same number
of suites and premium

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:37.050
but your media rights goes up 20-fold.

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:38.410
Promotion to the Premier League

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:42.393
could be worth anywhere from
130 to 200 million pounds.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.320
Just take this example.

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:48.180
In 2016 another second division team,

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:50.020
Wolverhampton Wanderers, were bought

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:52.480
for around 63 million dollars.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:54.580
They gained promotion shortly after that

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:56.150
and are now estimated to be worth

00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:58.810
around 10 times that figure.

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:01.200
Taking a punt, as they say in England,

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:04.683
on a team rising up the
divisions can really pay off.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:09.240
But what goes up, can also come down.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:12.310
A concept we Americans
aren't exactly familiar with.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:15.070
Two teams here who are
desperate for three points

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:16.630
to avoid relegation.

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:17.830
What are you looking forward to?

00:11:17.830 --> 00:11:20.490
Well, I'm looking forward to
the definition of relegation.

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:22.510
Three teams drop out of the Premier League

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:23.780
every season.

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:25.660
Yes, relegation is real and it's kind of,

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:27.210
you have to manage the delicate balance

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:30.411
because there is a correlation

00:11:30.411 --> 00:11:33.720
between acquiring more
players in the transfer market

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:35.920
and buying players to be able to,

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:38.110
to be more competitive.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:41.410
And winning and matriculating
up the table, right?

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:43.100
But at the same time
that comes with a risk

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:45.520
because if you spend a
lot in the transfer market

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:47.450
and you get relegated,
now all of the sudden,

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:50.090
you're potentially more
in a world of hurt.

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:52.520
So yeah, you do have to factor that in

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:53.620
and you do have to model that.

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:57.230
One year ago the club's
valuation was much different

00:11:57.230 --> 00:11:58.780
than it is today.

00:11:58.780 --> 00:12:00.070
And trying to figure out, okay well,

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:02.790
which one is the real,
sustainable valuation.

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:04.793
Like, that's a hard exercise to do.

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:06.967
I hope for Paraag

00:12:06.967 --> 00:12:09.960
and Leeds fans they
don't drop anytime soon.

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:11.740
The history of British
football is littered

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:14.860
with stories of big time clubs going down.

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:16.533
And not making it back.

00:12:17.994 --> 00:12:21.070
But for many fans a
relegation battle can be

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:24.683
just as exciting as the
chase for a championship.

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:29.880
It's a very performance-based system

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:32.507
and I'm sure there's
probably some fans that say,

00:12:32.507 --> 00:12:34.510
"Wow, I wish we had something
like that in America."

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:36.610
And I'm not suggesting any
owner wants that in America,

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:37.623
don't get me wrong.

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:42.040
Alan Pace and his U.S.
investment firm ALK Capital

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:44.310
have also just joined the Premier League.

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:46.010
They've taken over Burnley,

00:12:46.010 --> 00:12:48.680
a team just 50 kilometers away from Leeds

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:50.220
in the north of England.

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:53.343
We talk about performance-based
stuff all the time

00:12:54.366 --> 00:12:56.230
in business and as entrepreneurs

00:12:56.230 --> 00:12:57.550
and as other things.

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:00.997
Performance-based means you
get rewarded for performance.

00:13:00.997 --> 00:13:04.040
It seems kind of odd
that you wouldn't have it

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:07.317
within your own sports process of,

00:13:07.317 --> 00:13:10.177
"Look, if you don't perform,
you don't get to stay

00:13:10.177 --> 00:13:11.770
with everybody else."

00:13:11.770 --> 00:13:16.580
But it's also super interesting
and I think very cool

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:18.790
that people can get promoted

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:22.160
and people can get relegated
based off of performance.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:24.840
So Alan, as an investor,
what appeals to you

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:26.010
about the EPL?

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:28.050
You have a global audience.

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:31.860
That is very, very different
than any American sport.

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:35.950
I mean, you're talking a
billion plus people interact

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:37.560
with the EPL.

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:39.190
And when you look at any American sport,

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:40.420
there's nothing anywhere close to it.

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:43.010
Now, I'm not suggesting
that you've monetized

00:13:43.010 --> 00:13:44.820
that billion plus.

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:48.200
But if you just even
for half a second think

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:51.810
about how could I monetize that situation

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:53.410
from a business perspective,

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:54.860
you've got an audience already.

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:57.210
So, like, how do you bring that capability

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:59.140
and that knowledge from American sports

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:01.840
into an audience that's
just that much bigger.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:03.469
And, can you?

00:14:03.469 --> 00:14:05.760
That is a challenging
question for a lot of people

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:07.760
is can you actually do
something with that?

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:11.053
And honestly, our bet is yes you can.

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:17.390
What I have noticed
about football in Europe

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:19.750
versus, kind of, American
football in college,

00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:21.970
it's almost very similar.

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:25.170
That sense of community,
that sense of passion.

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:28.020
Everybody coming together
and really kind of jelling

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.570
for something, a big event.

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:32.250
And so, even though we're 30 - 40 minutes

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:34.350
outside of Manchester,

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:37.023
the whole community is a Burnley fan.

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.080
This is a relatively small town.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:42.560
Everybody knows everyone.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:45.363
And certainly they know
when an American shows up

00:14:45.363 --> 00:14:47.350
and buys the team.

00:14:47.350 --> 00:14:48.920
What does that feel like for you?

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:52.030
Well, you know, it's funny
because there's a lockdown

00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:54.290
and so we haven't really
got to meet a lot of people.

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:56.050
So no one's tried to
burn down my house yet.

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:58.200
And there's been no
pitchforks or anything.

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:02.860
There's no reason to believe
ALK will manage Burnley badly

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:04.840
but it's the kind of leveraged acquisition

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:06.430
that can make fans nervous

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:09.990
and has previously resulted
in major fan protest.

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:11.508
If I were a Burnley fan,

00:15:11.508 --> 00:15:15.056
I would be just a little bit
anxious, because you know,

00:15:15.056 --> 00:15:17.391
there I was supporting a club

00:15:17.391 --> 00:15:18.760
that was earning 100 million a year

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:21.723
from being in the Premier
League, from broadcast income,

00:15:21.723 --> 00:15:24.030
had 40 million pounds in the bank,

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:29.600
to a club that now has quite high debt.

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:31.840
They're still in relegation
trouble this season.

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:34.520
They could be in relegation
trouble most seasons.

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:36.050
And if they are relegated,

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:38.720
then obviously their
broadcasting income diminishes

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:40.030
by 20 times.

00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:43.020
Burnley could be relegated
and then never come up again

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:44.200
into the Premier League.

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:45.900
There's a definite risk of that.

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:48.820
If you're buying a team
in the Premier League

00:15:48.820 --> 00:15:51.770
the value of that team
is very closely tied

00:15:51.770 --> 00:15:53.740
to its on-field performance.

00:15:53.740 --> 00:15:55.890
And that is just not the case in the U.S..

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:59.700
You're de-risked to a great
degree as a franchise owner

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:00.533
in the States.

00:16:00.533 --> 00:16:03.140
Because you know if you have a poor year,

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:05.250
you'll get the top pick of the draft.

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:08.330
That gives you better chances of improving

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:09.570
in subsequent years.

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:11.520
You have one poor year
in the Premier League

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:12.880
and you're down a division

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:14.700
and losing a huge amount of revenue.

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.170
Teams adapted differently to relegation.

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:19.000
But some just go straight down.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:20.240
You know, they just can't cope.

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:25.103
It's like the whole fiber of
the club seems to collapse.

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:29.580
So Alan, I've looked at the message boards

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:32.620
and it's fair to say there's
some skepticism out there.

00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:34.770
Especially about the
structure of the deal.

00:16:34.770 --> 00:16:37.250
So from a lay person's perspective,

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:38.083
walk me through it.

00:16:38.083 --> 00:16:39.550
The best analogy that I would give as

00:16:39.550 --> 00:16:40.900
to what we've done is,

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:43.610
think about you've seen a house, OK,

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:44.750
that you really like.

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:46.020
But you think it's probably a little bit

00:16:46.020 --> 00:16:47.310
of a fixer-upper.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:50.170
And you take a mortgage
because it actually makes sense

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:52.220
with the process of what you're doing.

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:53.750
And then, you could use some

00:16:53.750 --> 00:16:56.120
of your capital more efficiently

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:58.890
in actually investing into
the club in different ways.

00:16:58.890 --> 00:17:01.270
Or fixing up the house in different ways.

00:17:01.270 --> 00:17:02.430
Yes, there's leverage.

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:03.360
There's no doubt about it.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:07.120
Because any of us that
have ever had a home

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:08.797
or that have ever charged
anything on credit,

00:17:08.797 --> 00:17:12.290
on a credit card, there's
leverage in our lives.

00:17:12.290 --> 00:17:13.720
Now if Burnley's a fixer-upper,

00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:16.650
this next club is one of
the most unlikely recipients

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:18.750
of an American makeover.

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:21.920
And we're not talking about
just any U.S. businessmen here.

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:23.910
We're talking about
A-listers, Ryan Reynolds

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:25.103
and Rob McElhenney.

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:28.210
Wrexham are what's known as minnows,

00:17:28.210 --> 00:17:30.160
small fish in a big pond.

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:32.310
In normal times they have
an average attendance

00:17:32.310 --> 00:17:34.123
of just 5000 fans.

00:17:34.123 --> 00:17:36.080
Which is actually pretty good

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:38.540
for a team in the fifth division.

00:17:38.540 --> 00:17:43.020
Last season they ranked 111th
in the football pyramid.

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:46.840
So, Wrexham is a long way from
that sweet broadcast revenue.

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:50.390
If you start in the bottom
you've got the amazing upside

00:17:50.390 --> 00:17:52.930
of the possibilities of promotion.

00:17:52.930 --> 00:17:55.210
Ultimately they, I think, are looking

00:17:55.210 --> 00:17:57.320
for a story to be able to tell,

00:17:57.320 --> 00:17:59.240
Ryan Reynolds and Rob McElhenney.

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:02.780
It looks like they are seeking
a vehicle for a narrative.

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:05.680
And ideally that narrative is:

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:08.338
Get the team promoted up the divisions.

00:18:08.338 --> 00:18:11.750
And that would make for
a pretty compelling show

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:14.640
on Netflix or Amazon Prime
or wherever it might land.

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:17.440
That team has not got a
huge amount of revenue.

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:20.710
Only sort of two or three
million pounds a year.

00:18:20.710 --> 00:18:22.400
If you're looking at a Netflix show

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:24.710
which you're selling for
several hundred thousand pounds

00:18:24.710 --> 00:18:27.400
an hour and you have an
eight hour mini series,

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:29.750
all of the sudden you've
doubled that team's revenue

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:31.300
in one fell swoop.

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:32.970
It's a sort of active management.

00:18:32.970 --> 00:18:35.030
It is very much bringing special sauce

00:18:35.030 --> 00:18:37.703
and applying it to the asset.

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:40.820
This isn't a punt either.

00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:43.450
In recent years streaming
services have been flooded

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:44.813
with sports documentaries.

00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:48.790
For most teams it's a
small part of the business.

00:18:48.790 --> 00:18:51.050
For Wrexham, according to a letter sent

00:18:51.050 --> 00:18:52.380
to potential sponsors,

00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:54.560
it's a key part of the commercial pitch,

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:56.810
along with the opportunity to
get your name on the shirt,

00:18:56.810 --> 00:18:59.470
the stadium and working personally

00:18:59.470 --> 00:19:01.683
with two interested Hollywood stars.

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:06.440
Sports documentaries cost
less than top level games

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:08.490
and have a longer shelf life.

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:09.380
The biggest problem is

00:19:09.380 --> 00:19:11.720
whether it makes compelling television.

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:14.600
Good TV requires troughs as well as peaks.

00:19:14.600 --> 00:19:17.000
Meaning losses as well as wins.

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:19.750
Next thing I know Rob
McElhenney gives me a buzz

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:22.340
and says, "I'd love to
pop up to Santa Barbara."

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.950
He, like all of us, started
to consume massive amounts

00:19:24.950 --> 00:19:26.400
of Netflix during lockdown.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:29.507
And stumbled across a
documentary series called

00:19:29.507 --> 00:19:31.390
"Sunderland 'Til I Die."

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:34.770
And he said, "I watched
this and I was enthralled."

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:38.090
And less with the football
action but the people

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:39.520
and the agony and the angst.

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:41.397
And he said, "I know these people.

00:19:41.397 --> 00:19:42.357
This is Philadelphia.

00:19:42.357 --> 00:19:46.417
This is every city that
is going on tough times,

00:19:46.417 --> 00:19:48.747
that is been let down by the one thing

00:19:48.747 --> 00:19:50.007
that can lift the spirits,

00:19:50.007 --> 00:19:52.040
which is their team."

00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:55.570
As a result he said, "I
called Ryan and said,

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:57.050
'We need to buy a football club.'"

00:19:57.050 --> 00:19:58.720
Just like I had to turn to the experts

00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:01.490
to really understand
football to begin with,

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:03.640
so too did Ryan and Rob.

00:20:03.640 --> 00:20:06.060
See, stars, they're just like us.

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:08.570
And they had the perfect guy.

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:11.160
Someone who'd not only
run a championship club

00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:13.340
but who'd actually lived in Wrexham.

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:14.400
Wrexham!

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:17.680
I grew up there, I played football there.

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:19.060
I played for Wrexham School Boys,

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:21.110
played at the Racecourse
Ground where Wrexham play.

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:23.660
So when I wasn't playing
I watched Wrexham.

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:27.000
And in those days they
were a very good team,

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:30.000
playing in the second
division of English football.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:31.470
'Giant Killers' as it's known.

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:33.490
So the big clubs would come in the FA Cup,

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:37.163
the knock out competition and
most famously beat Arsenal.

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:41.983
Fast forward to, you know,
as recently as a year ago

00:20:41.983 --> 00:20:44.570
and the club had fallen
into, quite frankly,

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:47.469
disarray, it'd gone into
administration, bankruptcy.

00:20:47.469 --> 00:20:48.511
Nolan.

00:20:48.511 --> 00:20:49.680
Well, he's tripped there.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:50.550
Now the situation

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:53.210
for Wrexham fans was kind of win-win.

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:55.940
They can't fall much
further and in their eyes

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:58.930
American ownership probably
means climbing the ladder

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:00.990
and maybe even hitting the big time.

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:02.640
Oh what a goal!

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:06.700
One of the goals of the season.

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:08.540
But when foreign owners pick up a team,

00:21:08.540 --> 00:21:11.880
in part to use it as a
vehicle for a video series,

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:14.905
it gives you a sense of how
much the game has changed.

00:21:14.905 --> 00:21:17.213
Football is a very simple virtuous cycle.

00:21:17.213 --> 00:21:19.960
You make as much money
as you possibly can,

00:21:19.960 --> 00:21:22.310
you invest that on the pitch.

00:21:22.310 --> 00:21:25.160
Those players bring you victories.

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:27.440
When you win a lot sponsors like that

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:29.680
and they will give you more money,

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:32.700
which you then turn around
and spend on the pitch

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:34.680
and you get even better players,

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:36.870
who win you league championships

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:38.430
and Champions League trophies.

00:21:38.430 --> 00:21:40.550
And sponsors wanna be
a part of that as well.

00:21:40.550 --> 00:21:41.630
And they give you even more money.

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:43.890
So it's a classic virtuous cycle.

00:21:43.890 --> 00:21:45.900
Ultimately what happens
in the best leagues

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:48.360
in the world won't have that much effect

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:49.610
on the grass roots.

00:21:49.610 --> 00:21:51.490
Kids will still go to the park and play

00:21:51.490 --> 00:21:54.450
and they'll continue to
play for years to come.

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:55.957
But the question remains,

00:21:55.957 --> 00:21:58.977
will big business spoil
the beautiful game?

00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:02.680
In recent months a lot
of chatter has emerged

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:04.920
about how the biggest clubs
in the world are trying

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:06.320
to consolidate power

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:09.130
and get a bigger slice of
the pie for themselves.

00:22:09.130 --> 00:22:11.037
Which raises another question,

00:22:11.037 --> 00:22:14.780
can English football survive
this Americanization?

00:22:14.780 --> 00:22:18.437
I think if you went to ask
almost any football fan,

00:22:18.437 --> 00:22:20.730
do you like relegation and promotion?

00:22:20.730 --> 00:22:23.330
They will say yes, because it is actually

00:22:23.330 --> 00:22:24.700
what brings a thrill to the game.

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:28.900
When people talk about
football evoking such passion,

00:22:28.900 --> 00:22:30.750
partly because there's
more at state for the club.

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:32.090
You know, if you lose a game,

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:34.980
you could be out of the top
league for a number of years

00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:36.990
and in the doldrums.

00:22:36.990 --> 00:22:38.090
There's a move to try

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:40.100
to get the top clubs in a league

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:43.590
which is always the same
members year in, year out.

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:46.950
It is a way of ensuring that
those clubs retain their value,

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:48.640
they have predictable revenue

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:52.000
that will be recurring
for the years to come.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:53.560
And because it's at a European level,

00:22:53.560 --> 00:22:56.640
whether we're playing against
all the other top clubs,

00:22:56.640 --> 00:22:59.150
they will attract a bigger,
international fan base.

00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:01.880
Now that might be to the detriment

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:06.370
of a normal fan, every day
fan, in their home city.

00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:08.070
For a simple reason, they may not be able

00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:12.410
to travel to Munich or Madrid
or Milan every other week,

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:13.480
it's quite expensive.

00:23:13.480 --> 00:23:15.850
And so it's something that investors like

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:17.563
but fans on the whole, really don't.

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:23.040
The biggest fear of course
is that you lose fairytales

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:24.410
like Leicester City.

00:23:24.410 --> 00:23:27.500
This is a team that rose
from being in the third tier

00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:30.440
of English football to
winning the whole thing

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:32.340
in a span of just seven years.

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:35.950
At 5000-1 to win the league that season,

00:23:35.950 --> 00:23:38.400
bookmakers had never paid out so much.

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:40.200
But there are other types of fairytales,

00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:42.060
maybe still to be written.

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:42.893
Who knows?

00:23:42.893 --> 00:23:43.909
Maybe we'll see Wrexham in the

00:23:43.909 --> 00:23:45.453
Premier League before too long.

00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:51.090
Thanks so much for watching.

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:53.100
We'd love to hear what
you think about this

00:23:53.100 --> 00:23:56.280
